issue(id="0002" status="closed")
  title Add structured parsing for Pug statement heads
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers formatter
    i-want statement heads parsed into structured syntax
    so-that formatting decisions can be based on actual Pug constructs instead of opaque line strings
  premise.
    The formatter currently preserves most non-comment, non-piped, non-raw
    lines as opaque statement text. That has been a good bootstrap strategy,
    but it blocks nearly every meaningful formatting improvement. A structured
    statement model should separate operator prefixes, tag names, shorthand id
    and classes, inline text, and attribute payloads while still preserving an
    escape hatch for syntax we do not yet understand. The goal is not to fully
    parse all of Pug in one step, but to establish a real statement AST that
    later tasks can build on safely.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Introduce a structured AST node for statement heads instead of storing most statements as opaque strings.
    criterion Parse tag names, shorthand ids/classes, and obvious inline text in a way that preserves existing idempotence on the generated docs corpus.
    criterion Preserve an explicit raw or unknown branch for syntax that is not yet modeled so the formatter can remain lossless while coverage grows.
    criterion Add focused tests covering plain tags, shorthand-only divs, doctype-like statements, and representative edge cases from the docs corpus.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") Operator-prefixed statements such as `-`, `=`, and `!=` remain intentionally deferred to task `0004`, while `0002` closes with a lossless raw fallback for unsupported heads.
    item(status="covered") Inline-text spacing remains preserved as-authored for now; normalization policy is intentionally left to later formatting tasks instead of being decided inside the parsing task.
    item(status="covered") Parsing and formatting remain separated by the current AST and formatter split so later width and whitespace policy changes can stay localized.
